{
  "title": "Mixed numbers with unicode fraction slashes corrupt ingredient \
item names",
  "description": "Some ingredients written as mixed numbers with the unicode \
fraction slash (`⁄`) are being split incorrectly, leaving the leading whole \
number as the parsed amount and shoving the fractional remainder into the \
`item` text.\n\
\n\
Concrete example from \
data/pipeline-snapshots/americastestkitchen-com_recipes-16187-sour-cream-and-onion-pasta.json, \
cross-checked against cached HTML at \
~/.ramekin/http-cache/americastestkitchen-com_recipes-16187-sour-cream-and-onion-pasta/response.bin:\n\
\n\
The source ingredient text is `1 1⁄4 teaspoons table salt , divided, plus salt \
for cooking pasta`, but the snapshot produces:\n\
  raw: `1 1⁄4 teaspoons table salt , divided, plus salt for cooking pasta`\n\
  item: `1⁄4 teaspoons table salt, divided`\n\
  measurements: [{amount: '1', unit: null}]\n\
\n\
The same recipe shows the same failure for `1 1⁄2 onions , sliced thin (1½ \
cups)` and `1 1⁄2 cups crushed kettle-style potato chips`, which become `item: \
'1⁄2 onions'` and `item: '1⁄2 cups crushed kettle-style potato chips'` with \
only `{amount: '1', unit: null}` captured in `measurements`.\n\
\n\
Expected behavior: normalize mixed numbers that use the unicode fraction slash \
the same way we already handle ASCII forms like `1 1/2`, so the full quantity \
lands in `measurements` and the `item` text starts at the actual ingredient \
name.",
  "status": "open",
  "priority": 3,
  "type": "bug",
  "labels": [
    "ingredient-parser",
  ],
